course: dictionary
lesson: 15426
title: Stance: how you stand and what you think
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, so look at the words around it to find the right one. «She took a wide stance at the crease, ready to hit the ball.» Here a stance is the way she placed her feet and body.
explain: «He took a strong stance on school uniform and said it should stay.» Now a stance is an opinion, the side he chooses. Ask yourself: is this about how someone stands, or what someone thinks? The clues tell you which meaning fits.
explain: Use the swap check. Put your meaning in place of stance. «He took a strong view on school uniform» works, so the meaning is opinion. Writers use stance when they want to show that someone has chosen a side firmly.
fact: Stance means the way someone stands, or their opinion on a topic.
fact: As a body position, a stance is how you place your feet and body, such as a batting stance with feet apart and knees bent.
fact: As an opinion, a stance is the side someone takes on a question, such as a stance in a class debate.
fact: Common phrases are «take a stance», «a strong stance» and «stance on».
fact: Sports words like batting or catching point to the body meaning; words about ideas, reasons or debates point to the opinion meaning.
exercise: «Sam kept a low stance, knees bent, ready to catch the ball.» What does stance mean here? | A fast run to the wicket | His opinion about the game | *The way he stood, with knees bent | A loud shout
exercise: «Mia took a clear stance: she believes school should start later.» What does stance mean here? | A quick jump over a puddle | The way she stands on one leg | A long walk to school | *Her opinion, the side she takes
exercise: Which phrase means someone has chosen a side on a topic? | *take a stance | sit in a chair | kick a ball | run a race
test: «The coach showed us a good stance for catching: feet apart and knees bent.» What does stance mean in this sentence? | A question we must answer | Our favourite food | *The way we stand and place our body | A loud cheer from the crowd
test: «Ben made a strong stance in the debate about litter.» What does stance mean in this sentence? | *The side Ben took, his opinion | A loud noise in the hall | A tall tree in the playground | A type of dance step
exam: «Priya took a firm stance on animal rights, and she gave three reasons.» What does stance mean here? | A pair of shoes | *Her opinion, the side she took | The way she stood on the stage | A picture she drew
